--TEST--
`Core\Cldr::pluralCategory` reads CLDR's operands from the digits the count shows, and refuses the two counts that show none it can read — `rule:programs/framework-core-half`, ADR 0054 § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// CLDR's `v` and `f` operands are the fraction digits a reader *sees*, so
// English's singular is `i = 1 and v = 0` and `1.0` is not it. A `decimal`
// carries its scale (ADR 0054) and answers that distinction exactly; an `int`
// has no fraction at all; a `float` is read at the digits it prints, which is
// what `echo` writes for the same value.
class Form {
    public static function of(int|float|decimal $count, string $locale): string {
        return match (Core\Cldr::pluralCategory($count, $locale)) {
            Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Zero => "zero",
            Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::One => "one",
            Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Two => "two",
            Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Few => "few",
            Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Many => "many",
            Core\Cldr\PluralCategory::Other => "other",
        };
    }
}

decimal $scaled = 1.0;
decimal $whole = 1;
echo "int 1: ", Form::of(1, "en"), "\n";
echo "float 1.0: ", Form::of(1.0 as float, "en"), "\n";
echo "decimal 1: ", Form::of($whole, "en"), "\n";
echo "decimal 1.0: ", Form::of($scaled, "en"), "\n";

// Czech reads the same fact from the other side: any visible fraction at all
// is `many` there, whatever the integer part is.
decimal $half = 1.5;
echo "cs 1: ", Form::of($whole, "cs"), "\n";
echo "cs 1.5: ", Form::of($half, "cs"), "\n";

// The two bounds, named on the side that is refused. A count that is not
// finite has no digits to read, and one whose digits outrun the operands
// cannot be evaluated over them — both throw rather than answering `other`,
// which is the answer a caller would have gone on to branch on.
float $vast = 1.0e300;
try {
    Form::of($vast * $vast, "en");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
// One past `u128`, which is where the integer part stops fitting.
float $wide = 1.0e39;
try {
    Form::of($wide, "en");
    echo "not reached\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
int 1: one
float 1.0: one
decimal 1: one
decimal 1.0: other
cs 1: one
cs 1.5: many
Core\Cldr::pluralCategory has no category for the count `inf`: it is not a finite number
Core\Cldr::pluralCategory cannot classify the count `1000000000000000000000000000000000000000`: it prints more digits than the plural rules are evaluated over
